      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think non-const reference arguments are generally not a good idea, and they\u0027re actually banned in the Google C++ style guide.  Readers of the calling code don\u0027t have a solid clue that the \"output\" argument is being modified.  IMO, it\u0027s better to either pass a pointer, which is a strong clue to client code that the arg may be modified or better yet in modern C++ style to return multiple values in a tuple.\n\nSo the interface might look like:\n\nstd::tuple\u003cbool, vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e\u003e decryptAes128Gcm(...);\n\nAnd the call site would look like:\n\nauto [success, plainText] \u003d decryptAes128Gcm(key, encryptedData, addlAuthData);\n\nThe functional notation -- args in, return value out -- is very clean.  The main downside is that at the call site the types of success and plaintText aren\u0027t always obvious.  Unless you pre-declare them, e.g.\n\nbool success;\nvector\u003cuint8_t\u003e plainText;\n[success, plainText] \u003d ....\n\nWhat might be even better is to use std::optional.  Like:\n\nstd::optional\u003cvector\u003cuint8_t\u003e\u003e decryptAes128Gcm(...)\n\nYou can then even do something like:\n\nif (auto plainText \u003d decrypteAes128Gcm(...)) {\n    // use plainText.\n} else {\n    // Handle decryption error\n}",
